    {
      "text": "{\"why\":\"The README's \\\"A Simple Example\\\" is the first thing the repo shows: a `Flask(__name__)` app with a `@app.route(\\\"/\\\")` view returning \\\"Hello, World!\\\". Since `flask run` would need a live server, the request is driven through Flask's own documented `app.test_client()` (docs/testing.rst), which exercises the exact same WSGI path: environ -> app context -> routing -> view -> response.\",\"script\":\"\\\"\\\"\\\"The README's minimal Flask application, exercised with one request.\\n\\nSave this as app.py and `flask run` would serve it; here the documented\\ntest client sends the request through the same WSGI machinery.\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\nfrom flask import Flask\\n\\napp = Flask(__name__)\\n\\n\\n@app.route(\\\"/\\\")\\ndef hello():\\n    return \\\"Hello, World!\\\"\\n\\n\\nif __name__ == \\\"__main__\\\":\\n    client = app.test_client()\\n    response = client.get(\\\"/\\\")\\n\\n    print(response.status)\\n    print(response.headers[\\\"Content-Type\\\"])\\n    print(response.get_data(as_text=True))\\n\\n    assert response.status_code == 200\\n    assert response.get_data(as_text=True) == \\\"Hello, World!\\\"\\n\"}",
      "type": "text"
    }
